  "query_preview": "..\n\nRidging aloft and at the surface continue to dominate the region and \nwill provide several more days of quiet weather around the Great \nLakes. The rex block will remain mostly intact the next couple days \nbefore slowly breaking down as the book end troughs break down in \nfavor of a more zonal pattern by the middle of next week. \n\nThe last few night fog has been the main concern in the forecast as \nis usually the case with ridging and flow off the Great Lakes this \ntime of year. Ample moisture with longer cool nights brings areas of \nfog across the state, heavily influenced by wind direct deciding \nwhich locations get the fog and how dense. As the center of the \nsurface drifts southward from southern Quebec toward the east coast \nof the conus, the surface flow will change from northeasterly to \nsoutheasterly today as we become positioned on the west side of the \nhigh. The change in flow will lead to the fog likely shifting from \nthe Thumb and Saginaw Valley down to the state line with the flow \ncoming off Lake Erie. Will keep the entry level patchy fog mention \nin for now and look more at it this afternoon. \n\nThe upper level ridge axis will cross the area Saturday night. This \nwill increase the southwesterly flow into the region allowing for a \nboost in temps Sunday. After slowly moderating upward through the \nmid 60s to end the week, Sunday could support highs in the 70s for \nat least part of the area. Looking into next week, a developing low \ntracking across Canada looks to send a weak backdoor cold front into \nthe region. The airmass is very dry with ridging across the SW conus \nblocking any moisture return, the front will offer some cooler temps \nas 850mb temps fall from the upper single digits C, down to just \nabove 0C behind the front with the next surface ridge.",
